export const LISTENING_PASSAGES: StepPassage[] = [
  { id: "l4", section: 'listening', title: "Train Station Announcement", text: "May I have your attention, please. The ten fifteen train to Alexandria has been delayed by twenty minutes because of a signal problem near Benha. It will now depart at ten thirty-five from platform four instead of platform two. Passengers holding tickets for this train may use the waiting room, where free water is available. The train to Tanta, however, is running on time and will leave from platform one at ten twenty. We apologise for the inconvenience." },
  { id: "l5", section: 'listening', title: "Booking a Hotel Room", text: "Man: Good afternoon, Seaview Hotel. How can I help you?\nWoman: Hello. I'd like to book a double room for three nights, starting on the twelfth of March.\nMan: Let me check. I'm sorry, we are full on the twelfth, but we have a room from the thirteenth.\nWoman: That's fine. How much is it per night?\nMan: A room with a garden view is ninety dollars, and a room with a sea view is one hundred and ten.\nWoman: I'll take the sea view, please. Does that include breakfast?\nMan: Yes, breakfast is included, from seven to ten thirty." },
  { id: "l6", section: 'listening', title: "Weather Forecast", text: "Good morning, and here is the weather for the weekend. Saturday will begin with heavy fog in the north, which is expected to clear by nine o'clock. After that, the day will be warm and sunny, with temperatures reaching twenty-eight degrees. On Sunday, clouds will move in from the west, and there is a strong chance of rain in the afternoon, so remember to take an umbrella if you are going out. Temperatures will drop to around twenty-two degrees. Next week looks cooler, with light winds throughout." },
  { id: "l7", section: 'listening', title: "Welcome to the Museum", text: "Welcome to the National History Museum. My name is Laila, and I will be your guide today. Our tour will last about ninety minutes and will cover three galleries. We begin in the Ancient Egypt gallery on the ground floor, where you will see statues, jewellery, and a collection of mummies. After that, we will go upstairs to the Islamic Art gallery. Please note that photography is allowed, but flash is not, because it can damage old objects. The tour will finish at the museum shop, where you can buy books and gifts." },
  { id: "l8", section: 'listening', title: "Two Students Talk about an Assignment", text: "Woman: Have you started the history essay yet, Karim?\nMan: Only the introduction. I thought the deadline was next Friday.\nWoman: No, Dr. Hassan moved it to this Wednesday, because the university will be closed on Thursday and Friday.\nMan: Oh no! That's only three days away. How long does it have to be?\nWoman: At least fifteen hundred words, and we must include five references.\nMan: I'd better go to the library tonight, then. Do you want to come with me?\nWoman: I can't, I have football practice. But I'll study with you tomorrow morning." },
  { id: "l9", section: 'listening', title: "Restaurant Special Offers", text: "Welcome to Palm Garden restaurant. Today we have three special offers. First, our lunch menu, served from twelve until four, includes a main dish, a salad, and a soft drink for only eighty pounds. Second, children under ten eat for free when they come with an adult. Finally, on Thursday evenings, we have live music from eight o'clock, and all desserts are half price. If you would like to reserve a table for the weekend, please speak to the manager at the front desk, as we are very busy on Fridays and Saturdays." },
  { id: "l10", section: 'listening', title: "Gate Change at the Airport", text: "Attention all passengers travelling on flight MS seven four two to Dubai. Please note that the departure gate has been changed from gate twelve to gate twenty-three. Boarding will begin at six forty and will close at seven ten, twenty minutes before departure. Passengers are reminded that only one piece of hand luggage is permitted, and liquids in containers larger than one hundred millilitres cannot be taken through security. Passengers requiring special assistance should go to the information desk near gate twenty. Thank you." },
  { id: "l11", section: 'listening', title: "Calling the Doctor's Office", text: "Woman: Good morning, Dr. Salma's clinic.\nMan: Hello, my name is Omar Farouk. I'd like to make an appointment, please. I've had a bad cough for about a week.\nWoman: I see. We have an opening this afternoon at three fifteen, or tomorrow morning at nine.\nMan: I work until five today, so tomorrow would be better.\nWoman: Certainly. That's tomorrow at nine o'clock. Please arrive ten minutes early to fill in a form, and don't forget to bring your insurance card.\nMan: Thank you. Is the clinic still on Park Street?\nWoman: No, we moved last month to Nile Avenue, next to the post office." },
  { id: "l12", section: 'listening', title: "A Short History of the Bicycle", text: "Good afternoon. Today I would like to talk about the history of the bicycle. The first machine of this kind was built in eighteen seventeen by a German inventor, Karl von Drais. It had no pedals; riders pushed themselves along with their feet. Pedals were added in the eighteen sixties in France, but these early bicycles were heavy and uncomfortable, and were nicknamed \"bone shakers\". The big breakthrough came in the eighteen eighties with the safety bicycle, which had two wheels of equal size and a chain. This design is almost the same as the bicycles we ride today." },
  { id: "l13", section: 'listening', title: "Radio Advertisement for a Language School", text: "Do you want to speak English with confidence? Join Bright Future Language School this autumn! Our friendly teachers have more than ten years of experience, and our classes have no more than twelve students, so everyone gets attention. We offer morning, afternoon, and evening courses to fit your schedule, and every student receives a free book on the first day. Register before the thirtieth of September, and you will receive a twenty percent discount. Visit us at the corner of Garden Street or call the number on your screen. Bright Future: your language, your future." },
  { id: "l14", section: 'listening', title: "Renting an Apartment", text: "Man: Hello, I'm calling about the apartment you advertised on Rose Street.\nWoman: Yes, it's still available. It has two bedrooms and a balcony, and the rent is five thousand pounds a month.\nMan: Is the building near public transport?\nWoman: Yes, there is a metro station five minutes' walk away. There is also a supermarket on the ground floor.\nMan: And is the furniture included?\nWoman: Only the kitchen. You would need to bring your own beds and sofas.\nMan: I see. Could I visit it this Saturday?\nWoman: Of course. Come at eleven, and I'll show you around." },
  { id: "l15", section: 'listening', title: "A Message from the Dentist", text: "Hello, this is a message for Mr. Nabil Samir from Bright Smile Dental Clinic. Your appointment on Tuesday the fourth at ten o'clock has to be moved because Dr. Mona is attending a conference. We can offer you Wednesday the fifth at two thirty, or Thursday the sixth at nine. Please call us back before Monday evening on zero two, five five five, one two three four to confirm which day you prefer. We apologise for the change and look forward to seeing you soon." },
  { id: "l16", section: 'listening', title: "Gym Orientation", text: "Hi, everyone, and welcome to Fit Zone. Before you start exercising, let me explain a few rules. The gym is open from six in the morning until eleven at night, every day except Friday, when we close at six in the evening. Please bring a towel and wipe each machine after using it. Lockers are free, but you must bring your own lock. If you are new to training, you can book a free session with one of our trainers at the front desk. Finally, please keep your phones on silent in the workout area." },
  { id: "l17", section: 'listening', title: "Campus Shuttle Bus Schedule", text: "Attention, students. From next Sunday, the university shuttle bus will follow a new schedule. Buses will leave the main gate every fifteen minutes between seven thirty and nine in the morning, instead of every half hour. In the afternoon, the last bus will leave the engineering building at five thirty, not six. The bus will no longer stop at the sports hall, but it will stop at the new medical faculty. Students must show their university card when boarding, and the service will not run on public holidays." },
  { id: "l18", section: 'listening', title: "Choosing a Major", text: "Woman: Have you decided what to study at university, Tamer?\nMan: Not really. My father wants me to study medicine, but I'm more interested in computer science.\nWoman: Well, medicine takes seven years. Computer science is only four.\nMan: That's true, and I enjoy programming. Last summer I built a small mobile game with a friend.\nWoman: That sounds impressive. Why don't you talk to your school counsellor?\nMan: I have an appointment with her on Monday. I'll also visit the university open day next week.\nWoman: Good idea. Whatever you choose, make sure it's your own decision." },
  { id: "l19", section: 'listening', title: "Road Closure News", text: "Here is the local traffic news. The Corniche Road will be closed to all vehicles from this Friday at midnight until Monday at five in the morning, because workers need to repair the surface after last month's heavy rain. Drivers travelling to the city centre are advised to use the ring road, although delays of up to thirty minutes are expected. Buses on routes twelve and twenty will follow a different path, and passengers can check the new stops on the city's website. The road will reopen on Monday in time for the morning rush hour." },
  { id: "l20", section: 'listening', title: "Visiting the Pyramids Site", text: "Welcome to the Giza Plateau. Before you begin your visit, please listen to some important rules. The site opens at eight in the morning and closes at five in the afternoon. Tickets to enter the inside of the Great Pyramid are limited and are sold separately at the gate near the Sphinx. Please do not climb on the stones, as this causes damage. Camel and horse rides are available, but please agree on the price before you start. Bottled water can be bought at the visitor centre, and we strongly recommend wearing a hat because the sun is very strong." },
  { id: "l21", section: 'listening', title: "A Job Interview", text: "Woman: Please sit down, Mr. Adel. Tell me a little about yourself.\nMan: Thank you. I graduated last year with a degree in business, and since then I have worked in a travel agency in Cairo.\nWoman: And why do you want to leave your current job?\nMan: I enjoy it, but I'd like more responsibility, and I'm interested in the international side of your company.\nWoman: Good. This position requires fluent English and some knowledge of French. Do you speak French?\nMan: I studied it at school for six years, but I'm still improving. I'm taking an evening course at the moment.\nWoman: Excellent. We will contact you by Friday." },
  { id: "l22", section: 'listening', title: "Lecture: How Volcanoes Form", text: "Good morning. Today we will look at how volcanoes form. Deep inside the Earth, the rock is so hot that some of it melts. This melted rock, called magma, is lighter than the solid rock around it, so it slowly rises towards the surface. When it finds a weak point in the crust, it can burst out, and once it reaches the surface we call it lava. Most volcanoes are found along the edges of tectonic plates, especially around the Pacific Ocean in an area known as the Ring of Fire. Next time, we will discuss how scientists try to predict eruptions." },
  { id: "l23", section: 'listening', title: "Supermarket Announcement", text: "Good evening, shoppers. The Green Valley Market will be closing in thirty minutes, at ten o'clock. Before you leave, please visit our bakery section, where all bread and pastries are now half price. Fresh fruit and vegetables are also on sale at the entrance, with a special offer on oranges: two kilos for the price of one. A reminder that plastic bags are no longer free; paper bags cost one pound each, and reusable bags are on sale near the checkout. We thank you for shopping with us and wish you a pleasant evening." },
  { id: "l24", section: 'listening', title: "At the Lost Property Office", text: "Man: Good morning. I think I left my backpack on the train yesterday.\nWoman: I'm sorry to hear that. What does it look like?\nMan: It's dark blue, with a red zip and a small yellow badge on the front.\nWoman: And what was inside?\nMan: A laptop, a notebook, and a water bottle. The laptop has a sticker of a football team on the lid.\nWoman: Let me check the list. Yes, a blue backpack was handed in at the Giza station this morning. You can collect it from there after noon with your ID.\nMan: Thank goodness. Thank you so much!" },
  { id: "l25", section: 'listening', title: "Healthy Eating Habits", text: "Hello, everyone. Today I would like to share three simple tips for healthier eating. First, try to eat five portions of fruit and vegetables a day. A portion is about the size of your fist, so it is easier than you think. Second, drink more water and fewer sugary drinks; one can of soda can contain up to ten teaspoons of sugar. Third, eat slowly. It takes about twenty minutes for your brain to realise that your stomach is full, so eating quickly often leads to eating too much. Small changes like these can make a big difference over time." },
  { id: "l26", section: 'listening', title: "Ordering a Pizza", text: "Woman: Roma Pizza, how can I help you?\nMan: Hi, I'd like to order two large pizzas for delivery, please. One chicken and one vegetarian.\nWoman: Certainly. Would you like anything to drink?\nMan: Yes, a large bottle of orange juice, and some garlic bread.\nWoman: That comes to two hundred and ten pounds. Delivery is free for orders over two hundred.\nMan: Great. My address is twenty-two Palm Street, apartment five.\nWoman: Perfect. It will be about forty minutes. And if it takes longer than an hour, the pizzas are free." },
  { id: "l27", section: 'listening', title: "Planning a Birthday Party", text: "Man: Sara's birthday is next Saturday. Shall we organise a surprise party?\nWoman: Great idea! Where should we hold it?\nMan: Her parents said we can use their garden. It's big enough for about thirty people.\nWoman: I'll make the invitations. Can you order the cake?\nMan: Sure. She likes chocolate, but she's allergic to nuts, so I'll tell the bakery to avoid them.\nWoman: Good thinking. What time should the guests arrive?\nMan: Her parents will take her out for dinner at six, so the guests should be there by six thirty to hide.\nWoman: Perfect. I'll tell everyone to keep it a secret." },
  { id: "l28", section: 'listening', title: "Interview with a Young Inventor", text: "Woman: Today we are talking to fifteen-year-old Youssef, who has invented a device that helps farmers save water. Youssef, how does it work?\nMan: It's a small sensor that goes into the soil. It measures how wet the ground is, and when the soil is dry, it sends a message to the farmer's phone.\nWoman: What gave you the idea?\nMan: My grandfather has a farm, and I noticed he often watered the crops when they didn't need it.\nWoman: And has it been tested?\nMan: Yes, on his farm for six months. He now uses about thirty percent less water." },
  { id: "l29", section: 'listening', title: "Cinema Announcement", text: "Welcome to Star Cinema. We would like to inform our customers that tonight's seven o'clock showing of the film \"The Last Voyage\" in Hall Two has been moved to Hall Four because of a technical problem with the screen. The start time remains the same. Tickets for Hall Two will be valid in Hall Four, and there is no need to exchange them. Customers who prefer not to watch the film can receive a full refund at the ticket desk until six forty-five. Popcorn and drinks are available at the snack bar, which is now offering a combined deal for fifty pounds." },
  { id: "l30", section: 'listening', title: "Instructions for a School Trip", text: "Good morning, class. Next Wednesday we are going on a trip to the Science Park. The bus will leave school at eight o'clock sharp, so please arrive by seven forty-five. You do not need to bring lunch, as it is included, but do bring a bottle of water and a notebook. Wear comfortable shoes, because we will walk for most of the day. Please remember to return the permission form signed by your parents by this Friday. Students who do not return it will not be able to come. We will return to school at three thirty." },
  { id: "l31", section: 'listening', title: "Lecture: Supply and Demand", text: "Good morning, everyone. Today we begin our introduction to economics with one of its most basic ideas: supply and demand. Demand means how much of a product people want to buy, and supply means how much sellers are willing to offer. When many people want something that is hard to find, its price usually goes up. For example, if a heavy storm destroys much of the tomato crop, tomatoes become scarce, and the price rises. On the other hand, if farmers produce too many tomatoes, the price falls. Next week, we will see how governments sometimes control prices. Please read chapter two before then." },
  { id: "l32", section: 'listening', title: "Choosing a New Phone", text: "Woman: Are you still using that old phone, Hany?\nMan: Yes, but the battery only lasts half a day now. I'm thinking of buying a new one.\nWoman: What's your budget?\nMan: About eight thousand pounds. I've seen two models. One has a better camera, but it costs nine thousand. The other has a bigger battery and costs seven thousand five hundred.\nWoman: I would choose the cheaper one. You said the battery is your main problem.\nMan: You're right. I take few photos anyway. I'll go to the shop tomorrow and compare them.\nWoman: Ask about the warranty, too. Some shops give two years instead of one." },
  { id: "l33", section: 'listening', title: "Using the University Library", text: "Welcome to the university library. I will give you a quick overview of our services. The library is open from eight in the morning until nine at night on weekdays, and from ten until four on Saturdays. It is closed on Sundays. You can borrow up to six books at a time for a period of three weeks, and you may renew them once, online or at the desk. The second floor is a silent study area, so please do not talk there, while group study rooms are available on the first floor and can be booked for two hours. Finally, printing costs fifty piastres per page." },
  { id: "l34", section: 'listening', title: "A Charity Run Advertisement", text: "Are you ready for a challenge? Join us on the twelfth of November for the Run for Hope, a five-kilometre charity race along the Nile Corniche. All the money raised will go to the children's hospital to buy new equipment. Runners of all ages and abilities are welcome, and there is a special short route of two kilometres for families with young children. The registration fee is one hundred pounds, and each runner receives a free T-shirt and a medal. Register online before the first of November, because places are limited to two thousand people. Together, we can make a difference." },
  { id: "l35", section: 'listening', title: "Asking for Directions", text: "Woman: Excuse me, could you tell me how to get to the central library?\nMan: Of course. Go straight along this street until you reach the traffic lights. Then turn left onto Jasmine Street.\nWoman: Left at the lights. Then what?\nMan: Continue for about two hundred metres, and you'll pass a big supermarket on your right. The library is just after it, opposite the post office.\nWoman: Is it far?\nMan: No, about ten minutes on foot. But if you prefer, you can take bus number nine from the stop over there.\nWoman: I think I'll walk. Thank you very much!" },
  { id: "l36", section: 'listening', title: "Lecture: Climate Zones", text: "Good morning. In today's class we will talk about the three main climate zones of the Earth. The first is the tropical zone, close to the equator, where it is hot all year and there is usually heavy rainfall. The second is the temperate zone, found between the tropics and the poles, where the weather changes through four distinct seasons. Finally, there is the polar zone, around the North and South Poles, where temperatures remain below freezing for most of the year. Egypt, by the way, lies mostly in a dry subtropical zone, which means that it receives very little rain. Please look at the map in your textbook on page forty." },
  { id: "l37", section: 'listening', title: "A Voicemail from the Manager", text: "Hello, Rania, it's Mr. Fathy. I'm calling about tomorrow's team meeting. Unfortunately, I have to change the time. It was supposed to be at ten, but I have an urgent appointment with a client, so we will meet at two in the afternoon instead. The place has also changed: please come to the third-floor meeting room, not the conference hall. I need you to prepare a short presentation on last month's sales figures, no more than ten minutes. If you have any problems, you can reach me on my mobile until six this evening. Thank you." },
  { id: "l38", section: 'listening', title: "Preparing for a Presentation", text: "Hello, everyone. Many people feel nervous when speaking in front of an audience, but good preparation can reduce that fear. First, know your topic well and organise your ideas into three clear parts: an introduction, the main points, and a conclusion. Second, practise out loud several times, ideally in front of a friend who can give you feedback. Third, keep your slides simple; use pictures and short phrases rather than long paragraphs. Finally, on the day itself, arrive early to check the equipment, and take a deep breath before you begin. Remember, the audience wants you to succeed." },
  { id: "l39", section: 'listening', title: "Returning a Faulty Product", text: "Man: Good afternoon. I bought this electric kettle last week, but it stopped working yesterday.\nWoman: I'm sorry about that. Do you have the receipt?\nMan: Yes, here it is. I paid for it on the fifth.\nWoman: Thank you. Our policy allows you to return a product within fourteen days. Would you like a refund or an exchange?\nMan: I'd prefer an exchange, please, but I'd like a different brand.\nWoman: No problem. The other brand costs two hundred pounds more, though. You'll need to pay the difference.\nMan: That's fine. Can I pay by card?\nWoman: Of course." },
  { id: "l40", section: 'listening', title: "Hotel Breakfast Information", text: "Good evening, and welcome to the Royal Palm Hotel. Breakfast is served every morning in the Garden Restaurant, on the ground floor, from six thirty to ten thirty. On weekends, it is extended until eleven. The buffet includes fresh bread, eggs, cheese, fruit, and traditional dishes such as ful and falafel. If you prefer to eat in your room, please fill in the card hanging on your door and leave it outside before midnight. Room service breakfast costs an additional sixty pounds. Please enjoy your stay." },
  { id: "l41", section: 'listening', title: "Tour Bus Announcement", text: "Good morning, ladies and gentlemen, and welcome aboard the city sightseeing bus. My name is Hossam, and I'll be your driver today. Our tour lasts two hours and has eight stops. You may get off at any stop and take the next bus, which arrives every thirty minutes. The first stop is the Citadel, where we will stay for twenty minutes, so please be back on the bus on time. Headphones for the audio guide are on your seats; you can choose Arabic, English, or French on the small control. Please keep your seatbelts fastened while the bus is moving, and enjoy the tour." },
  { id: "l42", section: 'listening', title: "Planning a Study Group", text: "Woman: Our chemistry exam is in two weeks. Should we form a study group?\nMan: Good idea. How many people do you think we need?\nWoman: Maybe four or five. Nada and Ali said they'd like to join.\nMan: Where should we meet? The library is too quiet to talk.\nWoman: What about the student café? It's noisy, but we could use the small room at the back.\nMan: Okay. I'm free on Monday and Wednesday evenings after six.\nWoman: Monday works for me, but I have a class on Wednesday. Let's start next Monday at six thirty.\nMan: Perfect. I'll create a group chat and send everyone the details." },
  { id: "l43", section: 'listening', title: "Lecture: States of Matter", text: "Good afternoon. Today's lesson is about the three common states of matter: solid, liquid, and gas. In a solid, the particles are packed tightly together and vibrate in place, which is why solids keep their shape. In a liquid, the particles are still close, but they can slide past one another, so a liquid takes the shape of its container. In a gas, the particles are far apart and move quickly in all directions, so a gas fills any space available. When we heat ice, it melts into water; when we heat water further, it turns into steam. Next, we will do a short experiment in groups." },
  { id: "l44", section: 'listening', title: "Opening of a New Park", text: "Local news now. The city council has announced that the new Al-Salam Park will open to the public this Saturday at ten in the morning. The park covers an area of twenty hectares and includes a lake, two playgrounds, a running track, and a small café. More than five thousand trees have been planted, and the council says that it hopes to plant another three thousand next year. Entry will be free for the first month, after which tickets will cost ten pounds for adults, while children under twelve will enter free. The park will be open every day from six in the morning until ten at night." },
  { id: "l45", section: 'listening', title: "Opening a Bank Account", text: "Woman: Good morning. How can I help you?\nMan: I'd like to open a savings account, please.\nWoman: Certainly. Are you a student or an employee?\nMan: I'm a university student. I'm twenty years old.\nWoman: Then you can open a student account. There are no monthly fees, and you receive a debit card. You need to bring your national ID, a proof of address, and a minimum deposit of five hundred pounds.\nMan: I have my ID and an electricity bill. Is that enough for the address?\nWoman: Yes, that's fine. If you'd like, you can also use our mobile app to check your balance." },
  { id: "l46", section: 'listening', title: "Welcome to the Online Learning Platform", text: "Welcome to the Smart Learn online platform. In this short video, I will show you how to get started. First, log in using the email address and password that you received at registration. On the home page, you will find all your courses; click on a course to open its lessons. Each lesson has a video and a short quiz, and you must score at least seventy percent to unlock the next lesson. If you miss a deadline, you can ask for an extension through the \"Help\" button. Finally, the discussion forum lets you ask questions, and a teacher will answer within twenty-four hours." },
  { id: "l47", section: 'listening', title: "Planning a Camping Trip", text: "Man: I've been thinking about our camping trip to Fayoum next month.\nWoman: Me too. How many days shall we stay?\nMan: Two nights is enough. We can leave on Thursday evening and come back on Saturday.\nWoman: Good. Who is bringing the tent?\nMan: Mahmoud has a big one that fits four people. I'll bring the sleeping bags and a small stove.\nWoman: I'll take care of the food. Should I buy water as well?\nMan: Yes, at least ten litres. It will be hot, so we'll need a lot. And don't forget a torch and a first-aid kit.\nWoman: I'll add them to the list." },
  { id: "l48", section: 'listening', title: "The Benefits of Walking", text: "Good evening, and thank you for coming to tonight's health talk. I'd like to speak about the simplest exercise of all: walking. Doctors recommend that adults should be active for at least a hundred and fifty minutes a week, which is only thirty minutes on five days. Walking at a fast pace strengthens the heart, helps control weight, and improves sleep. It also benefits the mind, as many people say a short walk helps them to think clearly and reduces stress. Best of all, it costs nothing and needs no special equipment, just a pair of comfortable shoes. So I encourage you to start today." },
  { id: "l49", section: 'listening', title: "In-Flight Safety Announcement", text: "Ladies and gentlemen, welcome aboard Flight six one nine to London. In a few moments, the cabin crew will demonstrate the safety features of this aircraft. Please fasten your seatbelts and keep them fastened whenever the seatbelt sign is on. The emergency exits are located at the front, the middle, and the back of the aircraft; please take a moment to find the exit closest to your seat. In case of a sudden loss of cabin pressure, oxygen masks will drop from above. Pull the mask towards you, place it over your nose and mouth, and breathe normally. The flight time today is approximately five hours." },
  { id: "l50", section: 'listening', title: "Lecture: Egyptian Hieroglyphs", text: "Good morning. Today we will look at the writing system of ancient Egypt: hieroglyphs. The word comes from Greek and means \"sacred carvings\". The Egyptians used more than seven hundred different signs, some representing sounds and others representing whole ideas. For many centuries, nobody could read them, until the discovery of the Rosetta Stone in seventeen ninety-nine. This stone contained the same text in three scripts, including Greek, which scholars could read. In eighteen twenty-two, a Frenchman named Jean-François Champollion finally explained how hieroglyphs worked. For everyday writing, the Egyptians also used a simpler script written with ink on papyrus." },
  { id: "l51", section: 'listening', title: "A Complaint about Noise", text: "Woman: Hello, Mr. Kamel. I'm sorry to disturb you, but I'm your neighbour from apartment six.\nMan: Oh, hello. Is there a problem?\nWoman: Well, last night there was loud music from your apartment until about two in the morning. I have to wake up at six for work.\nMan: I'm so sorry. My nephew was visiting, and I didn't realise how loud it was.\nWoman: I understand, but could you please keep the volume down after eleven o'clock?\nMan: Of course. It won't happen again. Thank you for telling me politely.\nWoman: Thank you. I really appreciate it." },
  { id: "l52", section: 'listening', title: "Podcast: Money Tips for Students", text: "Welcome to this episode of the Smart Student podcast. Today I'll share three tips for managing money at university. First, make a monthly budget. Write down how much money you receive and how much you spend on food, transport, and entertainment. Second, try to save at least ten percent of whatever you get, even if it's a small amount; it adds up over time. Third, use your student card, because many shops, cinemas, and museums give discounts to students. Finally, before you buy something, wait twenty-four hours. You might find you no longer want it." },
]

